1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,720
So, welcome to your introductory geography lecture. We'll begin with some basics.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,520
Firstly, what do we learn by studying geography?

3
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:14,510
Well, we learn a great deal about all the processes that have affected

4
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:17,580
and that continue to affect the earth's surface.

5
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,312
But we learn far more than that,

6
00:00:20,312 --> 00:00:27,338
because studying geography also informs us about the different kinds of relationships that develop between a particular environment

7
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:29,680
and the people that live there.

8
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:35,040
Okay. We like to think of geography as having two main branches.

9
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:38,080
There's the study of the nature of our planet

10
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:40,493
its physical features, what it actually looks like -

11
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:43,940
and then there's the study of the ways in

12
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:47,870
which we choose to live and of the impact of those on our planet.

13
00:00:47,870 --> 00:00:51,460
Our current use of carbon fuels is a good example of that.

14
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:54,882
But there are more specific study areas to consider too,

15
00:00:54,882 --> 00:00:59,330
and we'll be looking at each of these in turn throughout this semester.

16
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:01,438
These include bio-physical geography, by

17
00:01:01,438 --> 00:01:07,340
which I mean the study of the natural environment and all its living things.

18
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:12,750
Then there's topography- that looks at the shapes of the land and oceans.

19
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,625
There's the study of political geography and social geography too,

20
00:01:16,625 --> 00:01:20,500
of course, which is the study of communities of people.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:22,786
We have economic geography - in

22
00:01:22,786 --> 00:01:28,730
which we examine all kinds of resources and their use -agriculture, for example.

23
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:32,780
Next comes historical geography - the understanding of how people

24
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:40,880
and their environments and the ways they interact have changed over a period of time - and urban geography,

25
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:45,280
an aspect I'm particularly interested in

26
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,630
which takes as its focus the location of cities,

27
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:51,080
the services that those cities provide

28
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:54,380
and migration of people to and from such cities.

29
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:57,540
And lastly, we have cartography.

30
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:02,430
That's the art and science of map-making. You'll be doing a lot of that!

31
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:06,300
So, to summarise before we continue

32
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:10,052
we now have our key answer, studying this subject is important

33
00:02:10,052 --> 00:02:15,433
because without geographical knowledge, we would know very little about our surroundings

34
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:21,710
and we wouldn't be able to identify all the problems that relate to them.

35
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:28,150
So, by definition, we wouldn't be in an informed position to work out how to solve any of them.

36
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:34,630
Okay, now for some practicalities. What do geographers actually do?

37
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:37,610
Well, we collect data to begin with!

38
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:40,550
You'll be doing a lot of that on your first field trip!

39
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:44,000
How do we do this? There are several means.

40
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,260
We might, for example, conduct a census

41
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:50,330
count a population in a given area perhaps.

42
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:53,553
We also need images of the earth's surface

43
00:02:53,553 --> 00:03:00,000
which we can produce by means of computer-generation technology or with the help of satellite relays.

44
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,440
We've come a very long way from the early exploration of the world by sailing ships

45
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:07,318
when geographers only had pens

46
00:03:07,318 --> 00:03:09,060
and paper at their disposal.

47
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:14,300
After we've gathered our information, we must analyse it!

48
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,110
We need to look for patterns, most commonly those of causes and consequences.

49
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:23,560
This kind of information helps us to predict

50
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,420
and resolve problems that could affect the world we live in.

51
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,200
But we don't keep all this information confidential.

52
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,780
We then need to publish our findings

53
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:36,340
so that other people can access it and be informed by it.

54
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:41,560
And one way in which this information can be published is in the form of maps.

55
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:46,000
You'll all have used one at some stage of your life already.

56
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,310
Let's consider the benefits of maps from a geographer's perspective.

57
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:53,397
Maps can be folded and put in a pocket

58
00:03:53,397 --> 00:03:58,200
and can provide a great store of reference when they're collected into an atlas.

59
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,546
They can depict the physical features of the entire planet if necessary,

60
00:04:02,546 --> 00:04:06,530
or, just a small part of it in much greater detail.

61
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:08,320
But there is a drawback.

62
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:11,403
You can't exactly replicate something that is three-dimensional,

63
00:04:11,403 --> 00:04:17,184
like our planet, on a flat piece of paper, because paper has only two dimensions,

64
00:04:17,184 --> 00:04:22,580
and that means there'll always be a certain degree of distortion on a map.

65
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:23,910
It can't be avoided.

66
00:04:23,910 --> 00:04:27,900
We can also use aerial photographs

67
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,740
pictures taken by cameras at high altitude above the earth.

68
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:38,210
These are great for showing all kinds of geographical features that are not easy to see from the ground.

69
00:04:38,210 --> 00:04:48,670
You can easily illustrate areas of diseased trees or how much traffic is on the roads at a given time or information about deep sea beds, for example.

70
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:54,192
Then there are Landsats. These are satellites that circle the earth

71
00:04:54,192 --> 00:04:58,710
and transmit visual information to computers at receiving stations.

72
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:03,710
They circle the earth several times a day and can provide a mass of information -

73
00:05:03,710 --> 00:05:08,710
you'll all be familiar with the information they give us about the weather, for example.

74
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:13,640
So, what we're going to do now is look at a short presentation in

75
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:15,150
which you'll see all these tools...
